<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Damascus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Damascus is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, whose Great Mosque still stands there. It was later contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and served as a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> is buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damascus.jpg" alt="Damascus" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damascus,_Syria,_Panoramic_view_of_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Smbat's embassy to Karakorum brought back in 1250 a guarantee of Cilicia's integrity; Het'um I visited Möngke Khan in 1253 and fought under Hulagu at Aleppo and <b>Damascus</b> in 1259–1260, where Arab historians charge his troops with a massacre and arson in the main mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At first Saladin was held back by his nominal subordination to the Zengid ruler Nur al-Din in <b>Damascus</b>, but Nur al-Din's death in May 1174 freed him to expand on his own account.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghazan's victory at the Battle of Wadi al-Khaznadar in 1299, which briefly delivered <b>Damascus</b>, was their only major field victory in the conflict, and it was reversed at Marj al-Saffar in 1303.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Israel fought Aram-<b>Damascus</b> for decades over Gilead, and according to the Bible a king of Israel, traditionally Ahab, fell in battle at Ramoth-gilead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Acre became the kingdom’s greatest port and one of the busiest in the Mediterranean, where goods from <b>Damascus</b> and the East met Italian shipping.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Cities such as Cairo, Alexandria and <b>Damascus</b> became cosmopolitan centres of craftsmanship, scholarship and Islamic law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
